Whisper: hold, check and read-back for content clipboard writes

What: nsBaseClipboard::SetDataImpl, the one place every content clipboard write passes in
the parent process, calls mozilla::ClipboardGuard::Guard() for the first
(content-initiated) pass. The guard (new files in toolkit/components/clipguard) classifies
the text with the clipguard Rust crate and either lets the write through, removes a
trailing line break, or replaces it with a harmless placeholder while holding the real
text for "Copy anyway". The clipboard read paths (GetDataIfSmallerThanNative,
GetDataSnapshot, GetDataSnapshotSync) also ask the guard first: while a block is in effect,
a reader of the same origin as the writer reads back the page's own written text instead of
the placeholder, so the page cannot tell it was blocked; every other origin gets the
placeholder. nsBaseClipboard grants the guard and its event object friendship so they can
commit a held write through the existing SetDataImpl(aCheckContentAnalysis = false) path.
A real content write forgets the read-back override.

Engine-update contract (re-check on every Firefox rebase; the guard reaches into private
clipboard internals, so a change here can break it silently -- see the matching list and
the compile-time/runtime assertions in toolkit/components/clipguard/ClipboardGuard.cpp):
  1. SetDataImpl still takes aCheckContentAnalysis, and with it false neither re-runs the
     guard nor Firefox's content-analysis copy check, and commits synchronously.
  2. The write completion runs exactly once; the Handled path runs it and returns NS_OK
     without finish().
  3. PendingCopy / CancelPendingCopy and the mMutatingNativeClipboard re-entrancy guard
     still exist and behave as in 159 (bug 2057113, bug 2067877 last touched this).
  4. The three read methods above still carry the requesting window/principal.
Only a CI build and the smoke test exercise (2) and (3) end to end.
